### Summary 💡

Allow for semantic error handling with server actions.
This is a continuation of https://github.com/solidjs/solid-router/discussions/365 discussion, but focused on `solid-start` server actions.

The goal is to allow:
- return non-`200` (`401`, `500`, etc) responses from server actions for observability platforms to monitor how many responses are not `200`.
- handle such responses in code with reactive `useSubmission().error` when using without the form.

Proposal: use [Response.ok](https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Web/API/Response/ok) to populate `useSubmission().error`.

```tsx
const serverAction = action(() => {
  'use server';
  try {
    return perform();
  } catch(error) {
    // Error is serialised, no queries are revalidated (unless specified otherwise)
    // Response.ok is `false`, so `submission.error` is serialised Error.
    // This will make the action use to fail (as the error is rethrown), so needs try/catch
    return json(new Error('Something went wrong'), { code: 500 });
  }
});

const Component = () => {
  const performAction = useAction(serverAction);
  const submission = useSubmission(serverAction);

  // See example, QR Code scanning
  const userActionWithoutForm = () => {
    try {
      await performAction(); // same as fetch().catch() when the request has failed
    } catch {}
  }

  return (
    <Switch>
      <Match when={submission.pending}>Checking</Match>
      <Match when={submission.error}>Retry with another input</Match>
      <Match when={true}>Perform action</Match>
    </Switch>
  )
}
```

If the action returns non-`Error` data, the action does not fail:
```
// action, does not throw an Error
  return json('Something went wrong', { code: 500 });
// Component
  // similar to fetch(response => response.json()) without checking for response.ok
  await performAction();
  // rely on submission.error being populated with 'Something went wrong' string
```

### Examples 🌈

In the app, users can scan a QR code, when valid code is scanned – `action` is executed. If scanned code is invalid – error message is shown with Retry logic:
```tsx
const checkCode = action(async code => {
  "use server";
  try {
    return validity(code);
  } catch(error) {
    // ===Unclear===
  }
});
// component:
const submission = useSubmission(checkCode);
const checkCode = useAction(checkCode);

const onScanned = code => {
  try {
    await checkCode(code);
  } catch(error) {
    // do something, or just ignore it, same way we would do with using fetch directly.
  }
}

return (
  <Switch>
    <Match when={submission.pending}>Checking</Match>
    <Match when={submission.error}>Wrong code</Match>
    <Match when={true}><QRScanner onSuccess={onScanned} /></Match>
  </Switch>
);
```

### Motivation 🔦

Right now, to return non-200 response from a server action, one needs to use custom `Response`:
```ts
'use server';
return Response({ error: 'Something went wrong' }, { code: 500 });
```
This, however, makes `useSubmission(action).error` to be empty, because the response is a valid `Response`:

https://github.com/solidjs/solid-router/blob/e773947b85ac78281816e86621a2cdb6735ae95a/src/data/action.ts#L149

Making the `data` type from the action ambiguous: `{ correctResponse } | { error }`, which makes handling of the response complicated:

```ts
const performAction = useAction(serverAction);
const submission = useSubmission(serverAction);
const [error, setError] = createSignal(null);
const someUserAction = () => {
  try {
    const data = performAction();
    if (data.error) {
    // do nothing, rely on submission.result.error
    // but submission.error !== submission.result.error
    // by design to distinguish failures?
    } else {} // success logic
  } catch(error) {
    // will never get here
  }
}
```

So, another option is to make sure `submission.error` is populated, `throw` an error from the action.
```ts
const serverAction = action(() => {
  'use server';
  throw new Error('Something went wrong');
});
```
This works for `useSubmission().error`, but the HTTP Response status is now 200, so one needs custom metrics to monitor how the API is performing.

Doing `return json(new Error('error'), { status: 500, revalidate: [] })` is making action to fail itself (?). So `500`, but all queries are revalidated (?) and `submission.error` empty.

Follow-up promised in [#3600 (comment)](https://github.com/solidjs/solid/pull/3600#issuecomment-5791373769). Robustness fix; no public API change.

## Summary

While `sharedConfig.hydrating` is true, `createMemo` and function-form `createSignal` are the hydration facades. Both hand off to `hydrateSignalLike`, which peeks the next child id off `getOwner()!`. With no owner (`runWithOwner(null, …)`, or any detached creation) that is `childId(null, …)` → `TypeError: Cannot read properties of null (reading '_config')`, and the reactive system halts (`REACTIVITY_HALTED`).

#3600 fixed the one caller it hit (`useHead`'s `waitAsset` gate) by passing `{ transparent: true }` at the call site (`packages/web/src/client.ts`, `waitAsset`, ~L153–165). The general fix belongs in the facades: an ownerless node has no id counter to consume, so there is nothing to hydrate positionally and it should take the same non-hydrating path `transparent` already takes.

## Code (`origin/next` @ fd8b3df1c)

`packages/solid/src/client/hydration.ts`:

- `hydratedCreateMemo` (~L1268): `if (!sharedConfig.hydrating || options?.transparent) return coreMemo(...)`, else `hydrateSignalLike(coreMemo, …)`.
- `hydratedCreateSignal` (~L1275): `if (typeof fn !== "function" || !sharedConfig.hydrating) return coreSignal(...)`, else `hydrateSignalLike(coreSignal, …)`. Note this one has no `transparent` check at all.
- `hydrateSignalLike` (~L1154): the hybrid branch reads `peekNextChildId(getOwner()!)` (~L1196); the default path calls `hydrateSignalFromAsyncIterable` (~L764), whose first two lines are `const parent = getOwner()!; const expectedId = peekNextChildId(parent);`.
- `hydratedEffect` (~L1480) already has the `!sharedConfig.hydrating || options?.transparent` short-circuit, so effects have the escape hatch but memos/signals only honor it for `createMemo`.
- `lazyHydrationLookup` (~L1536) already does the null-safe version of this: `const key = o && o.id != null ? peekNextChildId(o) : undefined;`.

`packages/signals/src/core/owner.ts`: `childId` (~L220–226) dereferences `owner._config` unconditionally; `peekNextChildId` (~L260) is a thin wrapper.

Same-class siblings worth a look while in there (not required for this issue): `hydrateStoreLikeFn` (~L1371, `peekNextChildId(getOwner()!)`) and `hydratedCreateErrorBoundary` (~L1287, `const parent = getOwner()!`).

## Repro

Verified on a clean `origin/next` worktree with the hydrate vitest config (`packages/web/vite.config.hydrate.mjs`):

```tsx
/**
 * @jsxImportSource @solidjs/web
 * @vitest-environment jsdom
 */
import { expect, test } from "vitest";
import { createMemo, createSignal, runWithOwner, flush } from "solid-js";
import { hydrate } from "@solidjs/web";

function setup() {
  (globalThis as any)._$HY = { events: [], completed: new WeakSet(), r: {}, fe() {} };
  return document.createElement("div");
}

test("ownerless createMemo during hydrate()", () => {
  let thrown: any;
  const dispose = hydrate(() => {
    try {
      runWithOwner(null, () => createMemo(() => 1));
    } catch (e) {
      thrown = e;
    }
    return null;
  }, setup());
  flush();
  dispose();
  expect(thrown).toBeUndefined(); // FAILS on next: TypeError (reading '_config')
});

test("ownerless function-form createSignal during hydrate()", () => {
  let thrown: any;
  const dispose = hydrate(() => {
    try {
      runWithOwner(null, () => createSignal(() => 1));
    } catch (e) {
      thrown = e;
    }
    return null;
  }, setup());
  flush();
  dispose();
  expect(thrown).toBeUndefined(); // FAILS on next: TypeError (reading '_config')
});
```

Observed on `next`:

```
TypeError: Cannot read properties of null (reading '_config')
    at childId (signals/dist/dev-shared.js)
    at peekNextChildId
    at hydrateSignalFromAsyncIterable (solid/dist/solid.dev.js)
    at hydrateSignalLike
    at hydratedCreateMemo
```

Controls that pass on `next`: the same `createMemo` with `{ transparent: true }`; and the same ownerless `createMemo` outside `hydrate()`.

## Expected

An ownerless `createMemo` / function-form `createSignal` created while hydrating behaves exactly as if `{ transparent: true }` had been passed: it goes straight to the core primitive, consumes no hydration id, and never touches `_$HY.r`. No throw, no `REACTIVITY_HALTED`. Owned nodes are unaffected (the null-owner check is an additional short-circuit, not a change to the positional protocol).

Sketch:

```ts
function hydratedCreateMemo(compute, options) {
  if (!sharedConfig.hydrating || options?.transparent || !getOwner()) return coreMemo(compute, options);
  return hydrateSignalLike(coreMemo, compute, options);
}
function hydratedCreateSignal(fn, second) {
  if (typeof fn !== "function" || !sharedConfig.hydrating || second?.transparent || !getOwner())
    return coreSignal(fn, second);
  return hydrateSignalLike(coreSignal, fn, second);
}
```

(Whether the check should be `!getOwner()` or the `lazyHydrationLookup` shape `!o || o.id == null` — an owned node under an id-less owner has the same "no counter to consume" problem and today throws `Cannot get child id from owner without an id` from `childId` — is worth deciding once and applying to both.)

Once this lands, the `{ transparent: true }` at `waitAsset` becomes redundant but harmless; it can stay as documentation of intent.

— *Claude via Cursor*

A memo created with the (internal) `{ transparent: true }` option inside a component while `sharedConfig.hydrating` is true causes every element AFTER the creating component to fail its claim:

```
Hydration tag mismatch for key "2": expected <pre> but found [object HTMLElement]
[REACTIVITY_HALTED] ... TypeError: Cannot read properties of null (reading 'nextSibling')
Hydration completed with 1 unclaimed server-rendered node(s): <pre _hk="3" ...>
```

The unclaimed nodes stay visible but no client scope owns them — bindings in them are permanently dead. In an app the failure is silent-but-fatal: a footer rendered after such a component keeps its SSR appearance and never reacts to state.

## Minimal reproduction

vite 8.2.1 + vite-plugin-solid 3.0.0-next.27, solid-js / @solidjs/web 2.0.0-rc.0, `renderToStream` on the server, `hydrate` on the client. `src/App.tsx` is the whole story:

```tsx
import { createMemo, createSignal } from 'solid-js';

function Inner(props: { disabled: boolean }) {
	const pending = createMemo(() => false, { transparent: true });
	return (
		<nav>
			<button id="continue" disabled={props.disabled || pending()}>
				Continue
			</button>
		</nav>
	);
}

export default function App() {
	const [checked, setChecked] = createSignal(false);
	return (
		<>
			<label>
				<input
					type="checkbox"
					checked={checked()}
					onChange={(e) => setChecked(e.currentTarget.checked)}
				/>{' '}
				done
			</label>
			<Inner disabled={!checked()} />
			<pre id="after">sibling after Inner: {String(checked())}</pre>
		</>
	);
}
```

Observed:

- With the transparent memo: the `<pre>` fails to claim, hydration reports it unclaimed, the REACTIVITY_HALTED TypeError fires, and the checkbox no longer enables the button.
- Remove `{ transparent: true }` (or the memo entirely): hydration is clean and everything works.
- The memo's value never changes and is even never read in the broken variant — creating it is enough.

